Exact computational partial result for **holdout**, prepared with OpenAI Codex assistance on behalf of `fqlx`. I read this discussion before posting; it had no earlier comments. Upstream snapshot: `b8c4edeb80a82395ec07164caf445cfa43bbbd56`. The target and existing repository files are unchanged.

For the exact fixed `holdout` target, every certificate has homogeneous denominator degree at least **six**. The calculation allows arbitrary complex coefficients, arbitrary denominator parity, and a nonzero cleared quotient. It does not exclude any degree at least six and does not settle the instance. This is an exact-arithmetic mathematical partial result, independently reviewed and reproduced, but not a Lean theorem.

The target throughout is $q=1$, $A=X_0^3Y_0^3$, and
$$
F=X_0^6+X_1^6+X_2^6+Y_0^6+Y_1^6+Y_2^6+X_0^3X_1Y_0Y_1+X_1^3X_2Y_1Y_2.
$$
Write $Z=(X_0,X_1,X_2,Y_0,Y_1,Y_2)$, $F_i=\partial_i F$, $J=(F_0,\ldots,F_5)$, $N(F)=\sum_iN_iF_i$, and $B_D(N)=D\operatorname{div}N-N(D)$.

**Normalization and completeness of the degree bound.** A certificate satisfies
$$D(AD-N(F))+F B_D(N)=F^2Q.$$
Because $D$ is relatively prime to $F$, $F$ divides $AD-N(F)$. Put $R=(AD-N(F))/F$, which is homogeneous of degree $d$. The Euler field $\epsilon=\sum_i Z_i\partial_i$ gives a same-degree numerator replacement
$$N'=N+(R/6)\epsilon.$$
Euler's identities imply $N'(F)=AD$ and $B_D(N')=B_D(N)+DR\in(F)$. Thus this exact normalization preserves the denominator, its degree, and all permitted non-strict certificates. If $d<5$, multiply both $D$ and $N'$ by the nonzero monomial $h=X_0^{5-d}$; then
$$N''(F)=A(hD),\qquad B_{hD}(hN')=h^2B_D(N')\in(F).$$
The multiplied denominator remains nonzero. We only use the resulting normalized equations, and do not impose legality on that multiplied pair. Consequently, excluding every nonzero degree-five normalized pair excludes every certificate of degree at most five.

**The complete first equation in degree five.** Each $N_i$ now has degree six. The matrix of $(N_i)\mapsto N(F)$, with its $6\binom{11}{5}=2772$ columns $Z^uF_i$, $|u|=6$, has exact rational rank **2682**. Appending all $\binom{10}{5}=252$ columns $AZ^v$, $|v|=5$, raises the rank to **2902**. The projected space of possible $D$ therefore has dimension
$$252-(2902-2682)=32.$$
Exact Gaussian elimination constructs 32 independent denominator/lift pairs $(D_i,N^{(i)})$, each checked against $N^{(i)}(F)=AD_i$. The numerator-only kernel has dimension $2772-2682=90$. Separately, the 90 linear-coefficient Koszul tuples
$$Z_k(F_j e_i-F_i e_j)\quad(i<j,\;0\le k\le5)$$
are checked to have rank 90 and zero directional derivative. Therefore they span the **entire** first-equation fibre; this step needs no assumed regular-sequence or smoothness theorem.

**Necessary quadratic obstruction.** Write $D=\sum a_iD_i$ and choose the corresponding lift $N_0=\sum a_iN^{(i)}$. Every other lift is $N_0+K$, with $K$ in the Koszul space just described. The product rule gives $B_D(K)\in J$: mixed second derivatives cancel in $\operatorname{div}K$, and all remaining terms contain a partial $F_i$. Euler also gives $F\in J$. Thus a necessary condition for the second normalized equation is
$$[B_D(N_0)]=0\quad\text{in }(\mathbb Q[Z]/J)_{10}\otimes_{\mathbb Q}\mathbb C.$$
The exact script expands all 528 quadratic coefficient polynomials
$$C_{ii}=B_{D_i}(N^{(i)}),\qquad C_{ij}=B_{D_i}(N^{(j)})+B_{D_j}(N^{(i)})\ (i<j)$$
and reduces them modulo the full degree-ten Jacobian space (rank 1497). Row elimination in the 32 coefficient parameters identifies squared parameters as consequences of these equations. Since a square can vanish over $\mathbb C$ only when its parameter vanishes, four rounds leave respectively 23, 11, 8, and 5 active parameters. The ranks of these restricted quadratic systems are **224, 151, 34, 22**. No ansatz or symmetry condition was imposed.

Set
$$U=2X_0^3+X_1Y_0Y_1,\qquad H=Y_0^4-\frac{X_1^2Y_1^2}{12}.$$
The five surviving parameters $(a,b,c,d,e)$ give exactly
$$D=aX_0^2U+bX_0Y_0U+cX_0H+dY_0^2U+eF_{Y_0}/6.$$
Their remaining quadratic system has rank seven and is spanned by
$$
\begin{aligned}
a^2-be/3-cd/9&=0,& ab-de/6&=0,\\
ad+b^2/2+c^2/6&=0,& ae&=0,\\
bd+ce/3&=0,& cd&=0,\\
d^2+5e^2/12&=0.
\end{aligned}
$$
If $e\ne0$, the equations $ae=0$ and $ab-de/6=0$ force $a=d=0$, contradicting $d^2+5e^2/12=0$. Hence $e=0$, then $d=0$ and $a=0$. The remaining equation is $c^2+3b^2=0$. A nonzero denominator therefore has, after scaling,
$$D=X_0(Y_0U+tH),\qquad t^2=-3.$$
This is only a necessary family; it is not a certificate.

**Complete final test.** Over $K=\mathbb Q[t]/(t^2+3)$, the script tests this denominator using all 2772 numerator coefficients and all 126 degree-four quotient coefficients in
$$N(F)=AD,\qquad B_D(N)=FC.$$
This is the full normalized fixed-denominator system, with no restriction on its numerators or quotient. Representing $K$-linear algebra by rational blocks produces 5796 columns. The coefficient matrix has exact rational rank **5772**; appending its right-hand side raises the rank to **5773**. Thus it is inconsistent. Consistency over $\mathbb C$ would imply consistency over the coefficient field $K$ by Gaussian elimination, so this excludes complex solutions. The other sign of $t$ is its conjugate and has the same inconsistency.

As an independent final check, parametrizing all first-equation lifts by the 90 Koszul corrections reduces this same last test to 216 unknowns over $K$. Its rational block coefficient rank is **408**, and its augmented rank is **409**, again inconsistent.

**Reproduction and limits.** `verify_holdout_degree5.py` is a self-contained Python standard-library script. It performs all calculations with `fractions.Fraction`, constructs the exact target directly, generates the complete first-equation kernel, verifies all lift identities and Koszul dimensions, checks the elimination stages and seven residual equations, and builds the complete final system. It uses no floating-point values, numerical optimizer, degree truncation beyond the expressly stated $d\le5$ bound, parity assumption, or denominator ansatz. The script was run successfully and independently reproduced; runtime depends on the machine. All rational ranks are preserved on extension to $\mathbb C$.

The outcome is a low-degree obstruction only. No certificate, unbounded nonexistence theorem, or protected Lean acceptance statement has been proved. Degrees $d\ge6$ and other horizontal supports remain open. Accordingly, these results belong in the instance discussion, not a solve PR or `proof.json`.

Credit: the normalized certificate formulation and complete Koszul-correction obstruction come from Chapters 1 and 5 of the repository's mathematical exposition. The exposition credits [Benjamin E. Diamond](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29157) for the rational-vector-field certificate bridge, Griffiths for residue theory, and Claire Voisin for the surrounding invariant-coniveau motivation; the finite obstruction here uses the elementary algebraic normalization and correction identities. The degree-five specialization and exact calculations were developed and independently reviewed with OpenAI Codex assistance.


Background: [Euler normalization in Chapter 1](https://github.com/hodge-computer/sextics/blob/b8c4edeb80a82395ec07164caf445cfa43bbbd56/docs/01-certificates.md) and [complete correction map in Chapter 5](https://github.com/hodge-computer/sextics/blob/b8c4edeb80a82395ec07164caf445cfa43bbbd56/docs/05-computation.md). This calculation does not assume a geometric Hodge conjecture or infer validity from E1 alone.

For reproducibility, the complete standalone script follows. Save as `verify_holdout_degree5.py` and run `python3 verify_holdout_degree5.py` (tested with Python 3.11 and 3.14; standard library only). SHA-256: `81354d61d7a13931e76168fef766ac5c5fa0282e6c4fd69d704b27535060467e`.

```python
"""Standalone exact bounded obstruction for hodge-computer/sextics holdout.

Run: python3 verify_holdout_degree5.py
Uses standard-library rational arithmetic only. No Lean proof is claimed.
"""
from fractions import Fraction as Q
from itertools import combinations_with_replacement
import time

started=time.time()
A={(3,0,0,3,0,0):Q(1)}
F={
    (6,0,0,0,0,0):Q(1),(3,1,0,1,1,0):Q(1),
    (0,6,0,0,0,0):Q(1),(0,3,1,0,1,1):Q(1),
    (0,0,6,0,0,0):Q(1),(0,0,0,6,0,0):Q(1),
    (0,0,0,0,6,0):Q(1),(0,0,0,0,0,6):Q(1)}

def monomials(d,n=6):
    if n==1:
        yield (d,)
    else:
        for a in range(d,-1,-1):
            for u in monomials(d-a,n-1):yield (a,)+u

def add(a,b):return tuple(x+y for x,y in zip(a,b))
def padd(a,b,s=Q(1)):
    out=dict(a)
    for u,c in b.items():
        z=out.get(u,Q(0))+s*c
        if z:out[u]=z
        elif u in out:del out[u]
    return out

def pscale(a,s):return {u:c*s for u,c in a.items() if c*s}
def pmul(a,b):
    out={}
    for u,c in a.items():
        for v,e in b.items():
            w=add(u,v);out[w]=out.get(w,Q(0))+c*e
    return {u:c for u,c in out.items() if c}

def derivative(a,i):
    return {u[:i]+(u[i]-1,)+u[i+1:]:c*u[i] for u,c in a.items() if u[i]}
partials=[derivative(F,i) for i in range(6)]

def directional(N):
    out={}
    for i in range(6):out=padd(out,pmul(N[i],partials[i]))
    return out

def B(D,N):
    out={}
    for i in range(6):
        out=padd(out,pmul(D,derivative(N[i],i)))
        out=padd(out,pmul(N[i],derivative(D,i)),-1)
    return out

def reduce(v,basis,insert=False,comb=None):
    """Sparse exact column elimination, optionally tracking combinations."""
    while v:
        k=min(v);c=v[k]
        if k not in basis:
            if not insert:return v
            basis[k]=({i:e/c for i,e in v.items()},None if comb is None else {i:e/c for i,e in comb.items()})
            return None
        other,other_comb=basis[k]
        for i,e in other.items():
            z=v.get(i,Q(0))-c*e
            if z:v[i]=z
            elif i in v:del v[i]
        if comb is not None:
            for i,e in other_comb.items():
                z=comb.get(i,Q(0))-c*e
                if z:comb[i]=z
                elif i in comb:del comb[i]
    return {} if comb is None else comb

# Complete E1 kernel at denominator degree five: all N_i degree six.
rows11={u:i for i,u in enumerate(monomials(11))}
e1basis={};labels=[];denominator_pairs=[];numerator_nullity=0
for i in range(6):
    for u in monomials(6):
        k=len(labels);labels.append(('N',i,u))
        col={rows11[add(u,v)]:c for v,c in partials[i].items()}
        null=reduce(col,e1basis,True,{k:Q(1)})
        if null is not None:numerator_nullity+=1
assert len(e1basis)==2682 and numerator_nullity==90
for u in monomials(5):
    k=len(labels);labels.append(('D',u))
    col={rows11[add(u,v)]:c for v,c in A.items()}
    null=reduce(col,e1basis,True,{k:Q(1)})
    if null is not None:
        D={};N=[{} for _ in range(6)]
        for ix,c in null.items():
            item=labels[ix]
            if item[0]=='D':D[item[1]]=c
            else:N[item[1]][item[2]]=-c
        assert D and directional(N)==pmul(A,D)
        denominator_pairs.append((D,N))
assert len(e1basis)==2902 and len(denominator_pairs)==32
# Independently check that all numerator-only fibres are spanned by the
# 90 linear-coefficient Koszul syzygies, not merely assume regularity.
mons6=list(monomials(6));index6={u:i for i,u in enumerate(mons6)}
koszul_span={}
for i in range(6):
    for j in range(i+1,6):
        for u in monomials(1):
            K=[{} for _ in range(6)]
            K[i]=pmul({u:Q(1)},partials[j])
            K[j]=pscale(pmul({u:Q(1)},partials[i]),Q(-1))
            assert not directional(K)
            col={component*len(mons6)+index6[v]:c for component in range(6) for v,c in K[component].items()}
            reduce(col,koszul_span,True)
assert len(koszul_span)==90
Ds=[pair[0] for pair in denominator_pairs];Ns=[pair[1] for pair in denominator_pairs]
print('Complete E1 ranks: Jacobian=2682, augmented=2902, numerator kernel=90, denominator dimension=32')

# Reduce all quadratic E2 coefficient polynomials modulo J_10.
rows10={u:i for i,u in enumerate(monomials(10))};jbasis={}
for i in range(6):
    for u in monomials(5):
        reduce({rows10[add(u,v)]:c for v,c in partials[i].items()},jbasis,True)
assert len(jbasis)==1497

def normal(p):
    v={rows10[u]:c for u,c in p.items()};out={}
    while v:
        k=min(v);c=v[k]
        if k not in jbasis:out[k]=c;del v[k];continue
        for i,e in jbasis[k][0].items():
            z=v.get(i,Q(0))-c*e
            if z:v[i]=z
            elif i in v:del v[i]
    return out

pairs=list(combinations_with_replacement(range(32),2));coeffrows={}
for ix,(i,j) in enumerate(pairs):
    p=B(Ds[i],Ns[j])
    if i!=j:p=padd(p,B(Ds[j],Ns[i]))
    for row,c in normal(p).items():coeffrows.setdefault(row,{})[ix]=c
active=set(range(32));expected=[
    [13,17,21,23,24,25,26,29,31],
    [2,4,5,8,12,14,15,16,20,22,28,30],
    [7,9,11], [1,10,19]]
expected_ranks=[224,151,34,22]
for round_number,expected_forced in enumerate(expected):
    allowed={k for k,(i,j) in enumerate(pairs) if i in active and j in active}
    rb={}
    for row in coeffrows.values():reduce({k:c for k,c in row.items() if k in allowed},rb,True)
    assert len(rb)==expected_ranks[round_number]
    forced=[i for i in sorted(active) if not reduce({pairs.index((i,i)):Q(1)},rb)]
    assert forced==expected_forced
    print(f'Quadratic elimination: {len(active)} active parameters, rank {len(rb)}, forced squares {forced}')
    active-=set(forced)
assert active=={0,3,6,18,27}
allowed={k for k,(i,j) in enumerate(pairs) if i in active and j in active};rb={}
for row in coeffrows.values():reduce({k:c for k,c in row.items() if k in allowed},rb,True)
assert len(rb)==7
# Check that a specified set of seven equations spans this residual rowspace.
residual=[
    {(0,0):Q(1),(3,27):Q(-1,3),(6,18):Q(-1,9)},
    {(0,3):Q(1),(18,27):Q(-1,6)},
    {(0,18):Q(1),(3,3):Q(1,2),(6,6):Q(1,6)},
    {(0,27):Q(1)},
    {(3,18):Q(1),(6,27):Q(1,3)},
    {(6,18):Q(1)},
    {(18,18):Q(1),(27,27):Q(5,12)}]
residual_span={}
for equation in residual:
    vector={pairs.index(pair):c for pair,c in equation.items()}
    assert not reduce(vector.copy(),rb)
    reduce(vector,residual_span,True)
assert len(residual_span)==7
# Verify the remaining parameters' exact denominator meanings.
U={(3,0,0,0,0,0):Q(2),(0,1,0,1,1,0):Q(1)}
H={(0,0,0,4,0,0):Q(1),(0,2,0,0,2,0):Q(-1,12)}
assert Ds[0]==pmul(U,{(2,0,0,0,0,0):Q(1)})
assert Ds[3]==pmul(U,{(1,0,0,1,0,0):Q(1)})
assert Ds[6]==pmul(H,{(1,0,0,0,0,0):Q(1)})
assert Ds[18]==pmul(U,{(0,0,0,2,0,0):Q(1)})
assert Ds[27]==pscale(partials[3],Q(1,6))
print('Seven residual equations verified; their only nonzero projective possibilities have D=X0*(Y0*U+t*H), t^2=-3.')

# Full fixed-D normalized system over K=Q[t]/(t^2+3), represented as Q blocks.
D=(Ds[3],Ds[6])
def add2(a,b,s=Q(1)):return padd(a[0],b[0],s),padd(a[1],b[1],s)
def mul2(a,b):return padd(pmul(a[0],b[0]),pmul(a[1],b[1]),Q(-3)),padd(pmul(a[0],b[1]),pmul(a[1],b[0]))
def deriv2(a,i):return derivative(a[0],i),derivative(a[1],i)
n1=len(rows11);n2=len(rows10);nrows=n1+n2;fullbasis={}
def flat(p1,p2):
    out={}
    for pair,rows,offset in [(p1,rows11,0),(p2,rows10,n1)]:
        for part in range(2):
            for u,c in pair[part].items():out[part*nrows+offset+rows[u]]=c
    return out

def addcol(p1,p2):
    reduce(flat(p1,p2),fullbasis,True)
    reduce(flat((pscale(p1[1],Q(-3)),p1[0]),(pscale(p2[1],Q(-3)),p2[0])),fullbasis,True)

columns=0
for i in range(6):
    for u in monomials(6):
        mon={u:Q(1)}
        p1=(pmul(partials[i],mon),{})
        p2=add2(mul2(D,(derivative(mon,i),{})),mul2((mon,{}),deriv2(D,i)),-1)
        addcol(p1,p2);columns+=1
for u in monomials(4):addcol(({},{}),(pmul(F,{u:Q(1)}),{}));columns+=1
assert columns==2898 and len(fullbasis)==5772
reduce(flat(mul2((A,{}),D),({},{})),fullbasis,True)
assert len(fullbasis)==5773
print('Complete fixed-D system: 2898 K columns; 5796 Q block columns; coefficient rank 5772; augmented rank 5773: inconsistent.')
print(f'All exact checks passed in {time.time()-started:.3f}s. No d<=5 normalized pair exists; no unrestricted conclusion is claimed.')

```


**Further bounded degree-six exploration: unresolved.** I also generated the complete first-equation space for $d=6$ without imposing parity: 4,752 numerator columns have rank 4,437 (kernel 315), and appending all 462 denominator columns gives rank 4,790. The possible denominators form a 109-dimensional space. All 315 quadratic-coefficient Koszul tuples were checked independent and span every numerator-only fibre.

The same necessary obstruction modulo $J_{12}$ yields 5,995 symmetrized quadratic coefficient polynomials. Exact rational elimination has row rank 756 and forces eleven parameters to vanish; a second round has rank 652 and forces nine more. The residual system has 89 parameters and rank 589, with no remaining individual squared parameter in its row span. **That is the stopping condition, not a classification of its nonlinear zero set.** I did not solve that remaining system or the full variable-denominator second equation.

I separately tested each of the 109 chosen denominator basis vectors with its **complete** 315-dimensional numerator correction space and all 462 degree-six quotient coefficients. Every individual basis vector failed. This scan excludes those particular denominators; it does not exclude their arbitrary linear combinations. No degree-six impossibility result, higher-degree result, or certificate follows.

The second standalone script below reconstructs all of this from the exact target, with no external data files. Run `python3 verify_holdout_degree6.py --scan-basis`; omit the flag to skip the individual-basis scan. All arithmetic uses `fractions.Fraction`; the computation stops at the bounds just stated. SHA-256: `fe0315dc08ce6ef27b5e68e40a9569e9590b592928b61d2aeef01eff2b72be0e`.

```python
